A||N||4||Hawkman||Instrument panel lighting question...||||5||
Z||000000||Hawkman||04-25-2000||08:26 AM||||I just bought my 85 Fiero and have a question about the panel lighting brightness.<P>It seems that my panel lights seem quite dim even when adjusted to their highest level.  Is this normal?  Is there a way to make them brighter?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||04-25-2000||08:46 AM||||make sure all the bulbs work.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87ECO5||04-25-2000||09:03 AM||||I had the exact complaint about my '87's cluster.  Several suggestions were made, including brighter bulbs, painting the inner surfaces with a more reflective paint, even swapping out the gauges for the "preferred" backlit style.<P>Then, a week later, my alternator died!  After replacing it, my gauges were so much brighter, I actually had to dim them.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||04-25-2000||10:00 AM||||Fiero dash lights are a little dim in my opinion, but make sure the bulbs all work.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ozzy||04-25-2000||03:05 PM||||And for all those "Bright" sparks out there... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Exercise caution before placing brighter bulbs in behind the gauge cluster. More light means more heat! I read of a similar problem with the lit GT emblem. Melted plastic and such.<P>My GT does not cause sun burn but is sufficiently lit in my opinion.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
